.TH CCOL_DYNAMIC_QUEUE_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynamic_queue_create_with_mprocs \- create a dynamic queue with no size limit and with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_dynamic_queue *ccol_dynamic_queue_create_with_mprocs(
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function creates a queue with no size limit. A doubly-linked list
holds the queue. A send never waits for free space. It fails in three
cases: an allocation fails, the queue already holds ccol_max_elem_count
messages, or a ccol_dynmq_disable_sending(3) call turned sends off. A new
queue starts with sends turned on.

Every allocation of the queue goes through mmgmt_procs. This is true at
creation time and for each list node that a later send adds. The queue
keeps its own copy of those procedures and frees itself through them. The
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t of the caller therefore does not need to live longer
than this call. A NULL value selects the default malloc and free, which is
what the ccol_dynamic_queue_create() macro does.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mmgmt_procs  Custom memory management procedures, or NULL to use the
             default malloc and free. If the value is not NULL, all of
             malloc, calloc, realloc and free must be set.
err_str      Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure. Pass
             NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives a pointer to the new dynamic queue. If
err_str is not NULL, the function sets *err_str to NULL.

On failure, the function gives NULL. If err_str is not NULL, *err_str then
describes the reason. The reasons for a failure are: mmgmt_procs is
malformed, because it sets some but not all of malloc, calloc, realloc and
free; an allocation failed; the function could not initialize the mutex of
the queue or its condition variable; or, in a build that has fork-safety
protection compiled in, the function could not register the mutex of the
queue with the fork-safety registry. That registration also fails once the
process-exit destructors of the library have run and the last queue is
gone, for example in an application destructor that runs after them. None of these paths leaves anything
allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe.
A send never blocks. It fails instead of a wait in three cases: the queue
holds ccol_max_elem_count messages, the memory runs out, or sends on the
queue are turned off.
You must destroy the queue with ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy() after you
finish with it.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with this queue. The first
way is to call fork(2) before you create the queue. The second way is to
create the queue, then call fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One
pattern is not supported: a program continues to run with no exec(3) in
between while a queue from before the fork is still live. On top of that
rule, the library adds a defense in depth. The child never inherits a lock
in the locked state from another thread that held it at the instant of the
fork. Also, the queue owns no thread of its own. A queue that a child
inherits in that way therefore stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
